
Unique and Keyed Predicates
LogTScript supports special predicate names ending in $ and $$ for storing state with uniqueness semantics.
Quick reference

Predicate
Semantics

foo(...)
Ordinary predicate — multiple facts and duplicates allowed

foo$(...)
Single-valued predicate — only one active fact

foo$$(Key, ...)
Keyed predicate — one active fact per key


$ and $$ are part of the predicate name. They are not modifiers.
Therefore:
logts
pos(10)
pos$(10)
pos$$(player, 10)

are three different predicates.

───

Single-valued predicates — $
A predicate ending in $ may have only one active fact.
logts
pos$(10)
pos$(22)
pos$(35)

The effective knowledge base contains:
logts
pos$(35)

The latest fact replaces the previous value.
This applies regardless of arity:
logts
state$(idle)
state$(running)

or:
logts
state$(machine1, running)
state$(machine2, stopped)

The entire predicate signature is single-valued, so the second fact replaces the first.

───

Keyed predicates — $$
A predicate ending in $$ stores one value per key.
The first argument is always the key:
logts
property$$(Key, Value1, Value2, ...)

For example:
logts
property$$(boardwalk, 400, 50)
property$$(boardwalk, 450, 60)
property$$(parkplace, 350, 35)

The second boardwalk fact replaces the first:
logts
property$$(boardwalk, 450, 60)
property$$(parkplace, 350, 35)

Different keys coexist:
logts
position$$(player1, 10)
position$$(player2, 25)
position$$(player3, 7)

Updating player1:
logts
+ position$$(player1, 15)

produces:
logts
position$$(player1, 15)
position$$(player2, 25)
position$$(player3, 7)


───

Compound keys
The key is a normal ground logic term, so compound terms can be used as keys:
logts
playerState$$(player(id(10), team(red)), 100, alive)
playerState$$(player(id(10), team(red)), 80, alive)

The second fact replaces the first because the two key terms are equal.
A different compound key represents a different entry:
logts
playerState$$(player(id(10), team(blue)), 80, alive)


───

Runtime mutations
The same semantics apply to logic { + / - } mutations.
logts
logic {
    + position$(10)
    + position$(20)
}

leaves:
logts
position$(20)

For keyed predicates:
logts
logic {
    + position$$(john, 10)
    + position$$(mary, 20)
    + position$$(john, 30)
}

leaves:
logts
position$$(john, 30)
position$$(mary, 20)

The replacement happens as part of the transaction.

───

Rules
$ and $$ are also part of the predicate name when defining rules.
For example:
logts
position$(X) <-
    player(X)

and:
logts
position$$(X, Y) <-
    playerPosition(X, Y)

produce facts belonging to the corresponding unique predicate.
For $$, the first argument of each produced fact is its key.

───

Typical uses
Single-valued predicates are useful for global state:
logts
turn$(player1)
score$(100)
gameState$(running)

Keyed predicates are useful for entity state:
logts
position$$(player1, 15)
health$$(player1, 80)
money$$(player1, 500)

position$$(player2, 27)
health$$(player2, 100)
money$$(player2, 350)

This provides database-like state replacement while keeping the same fact/rule/mutation model used by the rest of LogTScript.
